<body>
    <h1>01 Spring boot test</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <h1 id=3D"id-01Springboottest-Component=EB=B3=84=ED=85=8C=EC=8A=A4=
=ED=8A=B8">Component=EB=B3=84 =ED=85=8C=EC=8A=A4=ED=8A=B8</h1>
<ul>
<li>Controller - integration test</li>
<li>Service - unit test</li>
<li>Repository - integration test</li>
</ul>
<h1 id=3D"id-01Springboottest-=EC=B0=B8=EA=B3=A0=EC=82=AC=ED=95=AD">=EC=B0=
=B8=EA=B3=A0 =EC=82=AC=ED=95=AD</h1>
<ul>
<li><a class=3D"external-link" href=3D"https://www.baeldung.com/spring-boot=
-testing" rel=3D"nofollow">https://www.baeldung.com/spring-boot-testing</a>
<ul>
<li>Controller
<ul>
<li>Integration testing with @SpringBootTest
<ul>
<li><p>As we=E2=80=99ve seen in the previous section, a test annotated with=
<span>&nbsp;</span><em>@SpringBootTest</em><span>&nbsp;</span>will bootstra=
p the full application context, which means we can<span>&nbsp;</span><em>@A=
utowire</em><span>&nbsp;</span>any bean that=E2=80=99s picked up by compone=
nt scanning into our test:</p></li>
<li><span style=3D"color: rgb(0,0,0);">However, we might want to avoid boot=
strapping the real application context but use a special test configuration=
. We can achieve this with the&nbsp;</span><em>@TestConfiguration</em><span=
 style=3D"color: rgb(0,0,0);"><span>&nbsp;</span>annotation.<span>&nbsp;</s=
pan></span></li>
</ul></li>
<li>Controller unit test - Unit testing with @WebMvcTest</li>
</ul></li>
<li><p>Service - unit test - Mocking with @MockBean</p></li>
<li>Repository - integration test - Integration testing with @DataJpaTest</=
li>
<li>Test seperation -&nbsp;<strong>Ideally, we should keep the integration =
tests separated from the unit tests and should not run along with the unit =
tests.</strong><span style=3D"color: rgb(0,0,0);"><span>&nbsp;</span>We can=
 do this by using a different profile to only run the integration tests</sp=
an></li>
</ul></li>
<li><a class=3D"external-link" href=3D"https://courses.baeldung.com/courses=
/1295711/lectures/30127904" rel=3D"nofollow">https://courses.baeldung.com/c=
ourses/1295711/lectures/30127904</a>
<ul>
<li><p><span style=3D"color: rgb(0,0,0);">Let=E2=80=99s quickly analyze the=
 kinds of tests we can perform in order to check the correct behavior of th=
e repositories=E2=80=99 methods.</span></p><p><span style=3D"color: rgb(0,0=
,0);">If we want to create Unit Tests, then we have to mock several JPA com=
ponents that Spring loads. Naturally, this would affect the functionality o=
f the repository. Therefore, Unit Tests don't make much sense in this case.=
</span></p><p><span style=3D"color: rgb(0,0,0);">On the other hand, when we=
 test Spring Data repositories, we usually want to verify that our queries =
retrieve the expected data from a database when running a SELECT operation.=
 When executing UPDATE, DELETE, and INSERT operations, we want to verify th=
at our queries get executed successfully, maintaining the consistent state =
of our database.</span></p><p><span style=3D"color: rgb(0,0,0);">Consequent=
ly,<span>&nbsp;</span></span><strong>we need integration tests,</strong></p=
></li>
</ul></li>
<li>unit test -&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://www.arhohuttunen.com/spring-boot-un=
it-testing/" class=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow">https://www.arhohuttu=
nen.com/spring-boot-unit-testing/</a></li>
<li>junit5 -&nbsp;<a class=3D"external-link" href=3D"https://blogs.oracle.c=
om/javamagazine/migrating-from-junit-4-to-junit-5-important-differences-and=
-benefits" rel=3D"nofollow">https://blogs.oracle.com/javamagazine/migrating=
-from-junit-4-to-junit-5-important-differences-and-benefits</a></li>
<li><a href=3D"https://dzone.com/articles/unit-and-integration-tests-in-spr=
ing-boot-2" class=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow">https://dzone.com/arti=
cles/unit-and-integration-tests-in-spring-boot-2</a>
<ul>
<li><p>Then, we have a little further problem=E2=80=A6The controller part i=
s the part of the system that manages HttpRequest, so we need a system to s=
imulate this behavior without starting a full HTTP server.<br><em>MockMvc</=
em><span>&nbsp;</span>is the Spring class that does that. It can be set up =
in different ways:</p>
<ol>
<li>Using Standalone Context</li>
<li>Using WebApplication Context</li>
<li>Let Spring autoconfigure it by loading all context by using these annot=
ations on test class @SpringBootTest @AutoConfigureMockMvc</li>
<li>Let Spring autoconfigure it by loading just the web layer context by us=
ing these annotations on the test class @WebMvcTest</li>
</ol></li>
</ul></li>
<li><a href=3D"https://github.com/HomoEfficio/dev-tips/blob/master/Spring-B=
oot-%EB%A0%88%EC%9D%B4%EC%96%B4%EB%B3%84-%ED%85%8C%EC%8A%A4%ED%8A%B8.md" cl=
ass=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow">https://github.com/HomoEfficio/dev-t=
ips/blob/master/Spring-Boot-%EB%A0%88%EC%9D%B4%EC%96%B4%EB%B3%84-%ED%85%8C%=
EC%8A%A4%ED%8A%B8.md</a>
<ul>
<li><p>=EC=BB=A8=ED=8A=B8=EB=A1=A4=EB=9F=AC=EB=8A=94 =EB=84=93=EA=B2=8C =EB=
=B3=B4=EB=A9=B4 =EC=82=AC=EC=9A=A9=EC=9E=90=EA=B0=80 =EB=B3=B4=EB=82=B8 =EC=
=9A=94=EC=B2=AD=EC=9D=84 =EC=84=9C=EB=B9=84=EC=8A=A4=EC=97=90 =EC=A0=84=EB=
=8B=AC=ED=95=98=EA=B8=B0=EA=B9=8C=EC=A7=80 =EB=AA=A8=EB=93=A0 =EA=B3=BC=EC=
=A0=95=EC=9D=84 =ED=8F=AC=EA=B4=84=ED=95=9C=EB=8B=A4=EA=B3=A0 =EB=B3=BC =EC=
=88=98 =EC=9E=88=EC=A7=80=EB=A7=8C, =ED=95=84=ED=84=B0, =EC=9D=B8=ED=84=B0=
=EC=85=89=ED=84=B0, =EC=9A=94=EC=B2=AD =EB=9D=BC=EC=9A=B0=ED=8C=85, =EB=B3=
